  "data": { "text": "Different levels of visual perceptual skills are associated with specific modifications in functional connectivity and global efficiency\n\nInternational Journal of Psychophysiology 123 (2018) 127–135\nhttps://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijpsycho.2017.10.002\n\nSabrina Danti, Giacomo Handjaras, Luca Cecchetti, Helen Beuzeron-Mangina, Pietro Pietrini, Emiliano Ricciardi\n\nTwenty-three young right-handed healthy subjects (13 M/10F; mean age ± s.d. = 26 ± 3 yrs) were enrolled in the study. All subjects signed a written informed consent prior to the enrolment into the study, under a protocol approved by the Ethics Committee of the University of Pisa, Italy (Protocol 1616/2003).\n\nThe task of the Mangina-Test consists of identifying a simple visual stimulus that is masked (inserted) into a simultaneously presented complex configuration, according to their exact geometrical properties: size, dimension, direction, spatial orientation and shape (Fig. 1). Five runs were obtained in each fMRI session.\n\nResponses to different stimuli were measured using blood oxygen level dependent (BOLD) contrast with the acquisition of T2*- weighted gradient echo planar images (GRE-EPI) in a 1.5 T GE scanner (General Electric, Milwaukee, WI). In each time series, the whole brain volume was acquired 108 times, each volume consisting of 33 contiguous 4 mm thick axial slices (TR = 2.5 s, TE = 40 ms, FA = 90°, FOV = 24 cm, IPR = 64 × 64 voxels and 3.75 × 3.75 mm). High-resolution T1-weighted spoiled gradient recall images (SPGR) (TR = 19.58 ms, TE = 3.58 ms, flip angle = 10°, FOV = 240 mm with 0.469 × 0.469 mm in-plane resolution, 512 × 512 acquisition matrix, and 1 mm axial slice thickness) were obtained for each subject to provide detailed brain anatomy; the SPGR images were used as an anatomical underlay for the statistical maps.\n\nData analysis was performed using the AFNI (afni.nimh.nih.gov; Cox, 1996) and Surf Ice. After slice time correction and spatial realignment, time series for each voxel were spatially smoothed (Gaussian kernel, 6-mm, full width at half maximum) and then normalized to the mean. All the volumes of the five runs were concatenated. The images were normalized to the Talairach-Tournoux space (Talairach and Tournoux, 1988) and resampled to 1 mm3 voxels.\n\nPreviously processed data were now treated to remove nuisance signals (i.e., head movements, the polynomial regressors accounting for linear, quadratic and cubic drifts): for each subject nuisance signals were regressed out from the voxel time series (3dSynthesize). Afterwards, data were low-pass filtered (3dFourier) at 0.15 Hz to remove high frequency physiological artifacts including cardiac and respiratory pulsatility (Birn et al., 2006; Danti et al., 2010; Hallquist et al., 2013).\n\nIndividual performances ranged from 37 to 44 correct answers (mean score ± SE = 40.8 ± 1.8).\n\nEach fMRI run contained eight stimuli pairs with two 15 s (or more, depending on response time) of rest at its onset and ending, to obtain a baseline measure of activity (270 s of total duration)." },
